        "content": "Now let me solve the problem carefully.\n\n**Part (1):** In △ABC, AB=3, BC=2√3, cosB=√3/3.\n\nBy law of cosines: AC² = AB² + BC² − 2·AB·BC·cosB = 9 + 12 − 2·3·2√3·(√3/3) = 21 − 12 = 9, so AC = 3.\n\nThen cosA = (AB² + AC² − BC²)/(2·AB·AC) = (9+9−12)/(2·3·3) = 6/18 = 1/3.\n\n**Part (2):** D on ray BA beyond A, DE∥BC, AE⊥AC, DE=√6, find CE.\n\nLet me set up coordinates. Place A at origin, AB along x-axis. Since D is on ray BA extended beyond A, the direction from B to A continues past A.\n\nLet A=(0,0), B=(3,0). cosB=√3/3, so angle at B. C is at distance BC=2√3 from B. Let me find C.\n\nVector BA = A−B = (−3,0), unit (−1,0). Angle ABC = B. C = B + 2√3(cos B · (−1,0) rotated...). Actually the angle between BA and BC is B. BA direction is (−1,0). So BC direction makes angle B with (−1,0). C = B + 2√3·(cos B·(−1,0) + sin B·(0,1)) = (3 − 2√3·(√3/3), 2√3·sinB).\n\nsinB = √(1−1/3)=√(2/3)=√6/3. So 2√3·sinB = 2√3·√6/3 = 2√18/3 = 2·3√2/3 = 2√2.\n\nx: 3 − 2√3·(√3/3) = 3 − 2 = 1. So C=(1, 2√2).\n\nCheck AC = √(1+8)=3 ✓.\n\nD on ray BA beyond A: ray from B through A extended, direction (−1,0), so D = (−t, 0), t>0.\n\nDE∥BC: BC vector = C−B = (1−3, 2√2) = (−2, 2√2). So DE is parallel to (−2,2√2), i.e., direction (−1,√2) up to scale.\n\nE = D + s·(−2, 2√2) for some s (with DE = √6). |DE| = |s|·√(4+8)=|s|·2√3 = √6 → |s| = √6/(2√3)=√2/2.\n\nAE⊥AC: AC=(1,2√2). AE = E − A = E. Condition AE·AC = 0.\n\nE = (−t − 2s, 2√2 s). Dot with (1,2√2): (−t−2s)·1 + 2√2s·2√2 = −t − 2s + 8s = −t + 6s = 0 → t = 6s.\n\nWith s = √2/2 (taking positive), t = 3√2. Then E = (−3√2 − √2, 2√2·√2/2) = (−4√2, 2).\n\nCE = distance from C=(1,2√2) to E=(−4√2, 2): Δx = −4√2−1, Δy = 2−2√2.\n\nCE² = (4√2+1)² + (2−2√2)² = (32 + 8√2 + 1) + (4 − 8√2 + 8) = 33 + 8√2 + 12 − 8√2 = 45.\n\nCE = 3√5.\n\nLet me verify with s negative: s=−√2/2, t=6s=−3√2 <0, invalid since t>0. So CE=3√5.",
